<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Introduction</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Welcome to the exploration of inertia! This fundamental concept in physics dictates how objects resist changes in their state of motion. Understanding inertia is crucial for grasping concepts like momentum, forces, and motion in general.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Definition</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist changes in its state of motion. This means an object at rest wants to stay at rest, and an object in motion wants to stay in motion with the same speed and direction, unless acted upon by an external force.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li><b>Resistance to Change:</b> Inertia is the measure of an object's resistance to acceleration.</li>
        <li><b>Mass as a Measure:</b> The mass of an object is a direct measure of its inertia. More massive objects have greater inertia.</li>
        <li><b>Newton's First Law:</b> Inertia is often referred to as Newton's First Law of Motion.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Types of Inertia</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Inertia can be categorized into three main types:
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Inertia of Rest</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Inertia of rest is the tendency of an object at rest to remain at rest. It resists any change that would set it in motion.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Inertia of Motion</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Inertia of motion is the tendency of an object in motion to remain in motion with a constant velocity (constant speed and direction). It resists any change that would alter its speed or direction.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Inertia of Direction</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Inertia of direction is the tendency of an object to resist changes in its direction of motion. An object moving in a straight line will continue to move in a straight line unless acted upon by a force that changes its direction.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Applications</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Inertia is observed in many everyday scenarios, from car crashes (seatbelts counteract inertia) to the spinning of a top.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Advantages</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Understanding inertia allows us to predict and control the motion of objects, leading to safer designs (e.g., car safety features) and more efficient systems.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Disadvantages</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Inertia can be a disadvantage in situations where we want to quickly change an object's motion. Overcoming inertia requires a force.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Important Notes</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>The concept of inertia is fundamental to understanding forces and motion.  Remember that mass is a direct measure of inertia.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Summary</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Inertia is the resistance of an object to changes in its state of motion. It has three main types: inertia of rest, inertia of motion, and inertia of direction. Understanding inertia is key to understanding how objects behave in response to forces and is a central concept in physics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Suggested Questions</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>How does mass relate to inertia?</li>
        <li>Give an example of each type of inertia in action.</li>
        <li>Why are seatbelts important?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
```

